[[:db/retract 17592186268594 :answer-field/choices 17592186268064] [:db/retract 17592186268594 :answer-field/choices 17592186266209] [:db/retract 17592186268594 :answer-field/choices 17592186266739] [:db/retract 17592186268594 :answer-field/choices 17592186266867] [:db/retract 17592186268594 :answer-field/choices 17592186268069] [:db/retract 17592186122534 :answer-field/choices 17592186122535] [:db/retract 17592186122534 :answer-field/choices 17592186122536] [:db/retract 17592186122534 :answer-field/choices 17592186122537] [:db/retract 17592186122534 :answer-field/choices 17592186122538] [:db/retract 17592186122534 :answer-field/choices 17592186122539] [:db/retract 17592186268639 :answer-field/choices 17592186267732] [:db/retract 17592186268639 :answer-field/choices 17592186267764] [:db/retract 17592186268639 :answer-field/choices 17592186266521] [:db/retract 17592186268639 :answer-field/choices 17592186267485] [:db/retract 17592186268639 :answer-field/choices 17592186267726] [:db/retract 17592186268590 :answer-field/choices 17592186268385] [:db/retract 17592186268590 :answer-field/choices 17592186268178] [:db/retract 17592186268590 :answer-field/choices 17592186266537] [:db/retract 17592186268590 :answer-field/choices 17592186267050] [:db/retract 17592186268590 :answer-field/choices 17592186266510] {:db/id 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-731abdd2-94a2-5d28-a3ea-af480d79c77c" "question-5c311fab-0276-5890-a0ca-a5fc54209d6b" "question-a2765af0-b76f-50a6-a22d-2203197bac13"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124269 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab5cb657-b1a4-53bf-b28c-dfde9b8dddbc" "question-9a747118-95ab-56ac-85bf-8bf99184999c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124366 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ is defined only when $\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) =  -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-efd3e6cc-b29d-5cd4-ba97-6a3820c7c7c6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124270 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}?$\n\nI. $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n\nII. $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n\nIII. $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268899 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268594 :answer-field/correct "answer-e8a1c753-6d41-529f-9c3d-fb5202e8c242" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8a1c753-6d41-529f-9c3d-fb5202e8c242" "answer-d65cece2-8603-5b9a-9d25-fed1938d13c9" "answer-046209a4-90cf-5839-90ba-12dff008cdd2" "answer-81eec312-e943-59cd-8a72-0a5450cde52a" "answer-5303e44c-4f5d-5836-a12b-fbc9787ef698"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8a1c753-6d41-529f-9c3d-fb5202e8c242" :answer/id #uuid "e8a1c753-6d41-529f-9c3d-fb5202e8c242" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d65cece2-8603-5b9a-9d25-fed1938d13c9" :answer/id #uuid "d65cece2-8603-5b9a-9d25-fed1938d13c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-046209a4-90cf-5839-90ba-12dff008cdd2" :answer/id #uuid "046209a4-90cf-5839-90ba-12dff008cdd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81eec312-e943-59cd-8a72-0a5450cde52a" :answer/id #uuid "81eec312-e943-59cd-8a72-0a5450cde52a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,z>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5303e44c-4f5d-5836-a12b-fbc9787ef698" :answer/id #uuid "5303e44c-4f5d-5836-a12b-fbc9787ef698" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-731abdd2-94a2-5d28-a3ea-af480d79c77c" :question/id #uuid "731abdd2-94a2-5d28-a3ea-af480d79c77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132636" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{1-{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}^{2} & <1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f0abe695-c967-560e-ae04-c016647dc51d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f0abe695-c967-560e-ae04-c016647dc51d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0abe695-c967-560e-ae04-c016647dc51d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-725590c3-4b9f-5868-9374-cd6d067e38ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fed3c9db-069d-50db-9968-81b70bc25ca6" "answer-725590c3-4b9f-5868-9374-cd6d067e38ed" "answer-80e1953c-36c5-5b8d-8d86-ba43cdf00921" "answer-bf77449c-16dc-501d-9410-b405c4d87435" "answer-fb238e8a-eb24-583f-ae9d-63514fd4cd34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fed3c9db-069d-50db-9968-81b70bc25ca6" :answer/id #uuid "fed3c9db-069d-50db-9968-81b70bc25ca6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-725590c3-4b9f-5868-9374-cd6d067e38ed" :answer/id #uuid "725590c3-4b9f-5868-9374-cd6d067e38ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80e1953c-36c5-5b8d-8d86-ba43cdf00921" :answer/id #uuid "80e1953c-36c5-5b8d-8d86-ba43cdf00921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\,\\text{and}\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf77449c-16dc-501d-9410-b405c4d87435" :answer/id #uuid "bf77449c-16dc-501d-9410-b405c4d87435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb238e8a-eb24-583f-ae9d-63514fd4cd34" :answer/id #uuid "fb238e8a-eb24-583f-ae9d-63514fd4cd34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c311fab-0276-5890-a0ca-a5fc54209d6b" :question/id #uuid "5c311fab-0276-5890-a0ca-a5fc54209d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132642" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2x+y},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n2x+y>0.\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2x+y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0406072-7d60-5b7c-9bcc-ab0dffa8c05b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0406072-7d60-5b7c-9bcc-ab0dffa8c05b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0406072-7d60-5b7c-9bcc-ab0dffa8c05b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3038239-88ed-52e2-9426-dfa5b47fafb1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7416925a-91b1-50ad-86f2-4722dc421da6" "answer-c3038239-88ed-52e2-9426-dfa5b47fafb1" "answer-9a9ac57f-3c30-59a7-a556-a7c62d0baac4" "answer-1790fef0-7d0d-55dd-bb76-6fc54ff729d4" "answer-e2ce14fd-f309-572e-8f19-4e9646a54602"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7416925a-91b1-50ad-86f2-4722dc421da6" :answer/id #uuid "7416925a-91b1-50ad-86f2-4722dc421da6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y>0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3038239-88ed-52e2-9426-dfa5b47fafb1" :answer/id #uuid "c3038239-88ed-52e2-9426-dfa5b47fafb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a9ac57f-3c30-59a7-a556-a7c62d0baac4" :answer/id #uuid "9a9ac57f-3c30-59a7-a556-a7c62d0baac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y\\ge 0\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1790fef0-7d0d-55dd-bb76-6fc54ff729d4" :answer/id #uuid "1790fef0-7d0d-55dd-bb76-6fc54ff729d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2ce14fd-f309-572e-8f19-4e9646a54602" :answer/id #uuid "e2ce14fd-f309-572e-8f19-4e9646a54602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124989 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x}{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122534 :answer-field/correct "answer-774aa4de-88d6-509c-a205-f19824043ae5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-774aa4de-88d6-509c-a205-f19824043ae5" "answer-01634dac-81d3-59c5-a62b-ca7f8454ed9c" "answer-2b7a3064-beed-5310-bb3c-85101dae7cda" "answer-c6e56c57-173c-51e9-9ecc-e613185f35cc" "answer-e3144809-7104-576d-84a5-3434485bdc8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-774aa4de-88d6-509c-a205-f19824043ae5" :answer/id #uuid "774aa4de-88d6-509c-a205-f19824043ae5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01634dac-81d3-59c5-a62b-ca7f8454ed9c" :answer/id #uuid "01634dac-81d3-59c5-a62b-ca7f8454ed9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b7a3064-beed-5310-bb3c-85101dae7cda" :answer/id #uuid "2b7a3064-beed-5310-bb3c-85101dae7cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6e56c57-173c-51e9-9ecc-e613185f35cc" :answer/id #uuid "c6e56c57-173c-51e9-9ecc-e613185f35cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3144809-7104-576d-84a5-3434485bdc8a" :answer/id #uuid "e3144809-7104-576d-84a5-3434485bdc8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2765af0-b76f-50a6-a22d-2203197bac13" :question/id #uuid "a2765af0-b76f-50a6-a22d-2203197bac13" :question/math-academy-id "q-36625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{xz},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $xz\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{y}{xz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{z}^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ xz & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $z$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-accd2032-6917-5f49-932d-3e1d41a7dbf8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-accd2032-6917-5f49-932d-3e1d41a7dbf8" :answer-field/id #uuid "accd2032-6917-5f49-932d-3e1d41a7dbf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec9c63dd-0d71-50dc-8f38-8d5bb5711a1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bc796fe-d62a-54b7-b62a-de399db7a42f" "answer-d659420e-783a-5c33-9fcd-6f355d4370a9" "answer-90ce7e6c-b66a-59fe-871e-d2e255747f96" "answer-71323b80-bf19-5934-b04d-4778dddfa219" "answer-ec9c63dd-0d71-50dc-8f38-8d5bb5711a1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bc796fe-d62a-54b7-b62a-de399db7a42f" :answer/id #uuid "4bc796fe-d62a-54b7-b62a-de399db7a42f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d659420e-783a-5c33-9fcd-6f355d4370a9" :answer/id #uuid "d659420e-783a-5c33-9fcd-6f355d4370a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90ce7e6c-b66a-59fe-871e-d2e255747f96" :answer/id #uuid "90ce7e6c-b66a-59fe-871e-d2e255747f96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71323b80-bf19-5934-b04d-4778dddfa219" :answer/id #uuid "71323b80-bf19-5934-b04d-4778dddfa219" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz<0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec9c63dd-0d71-50dc-8f38-8d5bb5711a1f" :answer/id #uuid "ec9c63dd-0d71-50dc-8f38-8d5bb5711a1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab5cb657-b1a4-53bf-b28c-dfde9b8dddbc" :question/id #uuid "ab5cb657-b1a4-53bf-b28c-dfde9b8dddbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72026" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ is the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a117f9f2-e34d-5807-9626-33ad39f12636"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a117f9f2-e34d-5807-9626-33ad39f12636" :answer-field/id #uuid "a117f9f2-e34d-5807-9626-33ad39f12636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-489da212-8c31-5076-b25d-dcb918ff12f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e377de25-0758-58a9-ac0e-ede3148fb64d" "answer-27de4785-aa81-5ec3-98bc-b5544ccafabb" "answer-489da212-8c31-5076-b25d-dcb918ff12f0" "answer-a196eecc-c2eb-5a98-9e76-115592f3cc76" "answer-4e05cd7a-d4fa-5955-a0e8-d22685cc7654"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e377de25-0758-58a9-ac0e-ede3148fb64d" :answer/id #uuid "e377de25-0758-58a9-ac0e-ede3148fb64d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27de4785-aa81-5ec3-98bc-b5544ccafabb" :answer/id #uuid "27de4785-aa81-5ec3-98bc-b5544ccafabb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y=0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-489da212-8c31-5076-b25d-dcb918ff12f0" :answer/id #uuid "489da212-8c31-5076-b25d-dcb918ff12f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a196eecc-c2eb-5a98-9e76-115592f3cc76" :answer/id #uuid "a196eecc-c2eb-5a98-9e76-115592f3cc76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne x\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e05cd7a-d4fa-5955-a0e8-d22685cc7654" :answer/id #uuid "4e05cd7a-d4fa-5955-a0e8-d22685cc7654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a747118-95ab-56ac-85bf-8bf99184999c" :question/id #uuid "9a747118-95ab-56ac-85bf-8bf99184999c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132660" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2}{2x-y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{2x-y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y)$ is defined only when $2x-y>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2}{2x-y}$ is defined only when $2x-y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2x-y}$ is defined only when $2x-y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a866ffa-79c2-5e4b-b2be-6e2a30da0437"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a866ffa-79c2-5e4b-b2be-6e2a30da0437" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a866ffa-79c2-5e4b-b2be-6e2a30da0437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b7b296ad-cbc4-5488-b508-efe5f3387f24" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d419f1b4-7533-5883-841b-add2b6c11aa6" "answer-b7b296ad-cbc4-5488-b508-efe5f3387f24" "answer-1f4f72ba-d36d-503e-ab8f-4442fb8cfa85" "answer-401ee91d-eebe-51cf-9a03-9bef082e025d" "answer-fc762ab1-6307-54fe-90c9-636cebc460e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d419f1b4-7533-5883-841b-add2b6c11aa6" :answer/id #uuid "d419f1b4-7533-5883-841b-add2b6c11aa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y>0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7b296ad-cbc4-5488-b508-efe5f3387f24" :answer/id #uuid "b7b296ad-cbc4-5488-b508-efe5f3387f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f4f72ba-d36d-503e-ab8f-4442fb8cfa85" :answer/id #uuid "1f4f72ba-d36d-503e-ab8f-4442fb8cfa85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2x-y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-401ee91d-eebe-51cf-9a03-9bef082e025d" :answer/id #uuid "401ee91d-eebe-51cf-9a03-9bef082e025d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc762ab1-6307-54fe-90c9-636cebc460e9" :answer/id #uuid "fc762ab1-6307-54fe-90c9-636cebc460e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268904 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268639 :answer-field/correct "answer-ac803e0a-8648-5814-b4da-1b0825992d84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-996e678e-be5f-503c-9a96-abb15e7826f4" "answer-706cf926-b949-5bdc-85d2-0e539e208835" "answer-964fbde3-4736-56ae-8abc-148f1494924e" "answer-ac803e0a-8648-5814-b4da-1b0825992d84" "answer-2c8a05de-b0b5-5169-8faa-237f89558e11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-996e678e-be5f-503c-9a96-abb15e7826f4" :answer/id #uuid "996e678e-be5f-503c-9a96-abb15e7826f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x<0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-706cf926-b949-5bdc-85d2-0e539e208835" :answer/id #uuid "706cf926-b949-5bdc-85d2-0e539e208835" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y<0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-964fbde3-4736-56ae-8abc-148f1494924e" :answer/id #uuid "964fbde3-4736-56ae-8abc-148f1494924e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac803e0a-8648-5814-b4da-1b0825992d84" :answer/id #uuid "ac803e0a-8648-5814-b4da-1b0825992d84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c8a05de-b0b5-5169-8faa-237f89558e11" :answer/id #uuid "2c8a05de-b0b5-5169-8faa-237f89558e11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268902 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268590 :answer-field/correct "answer-fcd66228-0a21-5760-a1d5-bdaa0fed4a8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17fd5860-a26a-576d-80ff-1b54ecf72a9c" "answer-d87caa01-6a77-556d-9fe7-0f71e1ac4555" "answer-fcd66228-0a21-5760-a1d5-bdaa0fed4a8c" "answer-2004c411-b459-5b77-a801-74476a6bdffc" "answer-f239db08-0717-5edf-8216-e8906fe4e2a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17fd5860-a26a-576d-80ff-1b54ecf72a9c" :answer/id #uuid "17fd5860-a26a-576d-80ff-1b54ecf72a9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d87caa01-6a77-556d-9fe7-0f71e1ac4555" :answer/id #uuid "d87caa01-6a77-556d-9fe7-0f71e1ac4555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcd66228-0a21-5760-a1d5-bdaa0fed4a8c" :answer/id #uuid "fcd66228-0a21-5760-a1d5-bdaa0fed4a8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2004c411-b459-5b77-a801-74476a6bdffc" :answer/id #uuid "2004c411-b459-5b77-a801-74476a6bdffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f239db08-0717-5edf-8216-e8906fe4e2a8" :answer/id #uuid "f239db08-0717-5edf-8216-e8906fe4e2a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186409558 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335822 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382214 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$\n\nI. ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ is defined\n\nII. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,0)$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(1,1)$ is defined" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-efd3e6cc-b29d-5cd4-ba97-6a3820c7c7c6" :question/id #uuid "efd3e6cc-b29d-5cd4-ba97-6a3820c7c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132672" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y} \\\\  & ={e}^{x/y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x})+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x/y}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/y}(2x+y)}{2y\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot {e}^{x/y}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\sqrt{1}\\,{e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/1}(2(1)+1)}{2(1)\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{3e}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}\\,{e}^{1/1}}{{1}^{2}}=-e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=\\frac{{e}^{0/1}(2(0)+1)}{2(1)\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\sqrt{0}\\,{e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(0,1)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{0}^{3}}\\,{e}^{0/1}}{{1}^{2}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3372d42-969f-5366-b1e8-185b07d6ec65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3372d42-969f-5366-b1e8-185b07d6ec65" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3372d42-969f-5366-b1e8-185b07d6ec65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fee3b5d2-fd96-5669-bf6b-ca44d5f343be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f86cf00-c816-5f72-b724-fc9da5c2a04b" "answer-fee3b5d2-fd96-5669-bf6b-ca44d5f343be" "answer-9ea1dfe8-25dc-5ae1-81c7-290025cbd773" "answer-cd447935-9b58-5094-b831-45d8525659d2" "answer-b87e8913-d11b-5f35-8c5c-1de5bded4f4f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f86cf00-c816-5f72-b724-fc9da5c2a04b" :answer/id #uuid "4f86cf00-c816-5f72-b724-fc9da5c2a04b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fee3b5d2-fd96-5669-bf6b-ca44d5f343be" :answer/id #uuid "fee3b5d2-fd96-5669-bf6b-ca44d5f343be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ea1dfe8-25dc-5ae1-81c7-290025cbd773" :answer/id #uuid "9ea1dfe8-25dc-5ae1-81c7-290025cbd773" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd447935-9b58-5094-b831-45d8525659d2" :answer/id #uuid "cd447935-9b58-5094-b831-45d8525659d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b87e8913-d11b-5f35-8c5c-1de5bded4f4f" :answer/id #uuid "b87e8913-d11b-5f35-8c5c-1de5bded4f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186381055 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}?$\n\nI. The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125741 :tutorial/content "The partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $f(x,y) = \\ln(xy).$ We wish to determine the set of values $(x,y)$ on which the partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists.\n\nFirst, we need to find the domain of $f$ since we cannot differentiate $f$ at any point that lies outside of its domain. The natural logarithm is defined only for positive inputs, so the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy > 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ (also denoted $f_x$) is not defined at $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_x})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>0\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, xy \\gt 0 \\}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125742 :tutorial/content "From single-variable calculus, if a function $y=f(x)$ is differentiable at a point, then it is continuous at that point.\n\nBut for a multivariable function, even if all partial derivatives are defined at a certain point, it **does not mean** that the function is continuous at that point!\n\nThe reason why partial differentiability does not imply continuity in the case of a multivariable function is that\n\n- the existence of the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ depend only on the behavior of $f$ parallel to the $x$- and $y$-axes, whereas\n\n- the continuity of $f$ depends on its behavior as we approach $(x_0,y_0)$ from any direction.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following multivariable function:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5aad599789d1e317ff3ddc07eaa418e80469307f67e98e2108660fc7d15701.svg)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined at the point $(0,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(0,0)=0\n$$\n\nLikewise, the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ are both defined at the point $(0,0).$ We can compute them using the limit definition of the partial derivative, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0+h,0)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(h)(0)}{{h}^{2}+{0}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,0) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[f(0,0+h)-f(0,0)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[\\frac{(0)(h)}{{0}^{2}+{h}^{2}}-0] \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1}{h}[0-0] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the partial derivatives both exist at $(0,0).$ We will now show that the function is not continuous at $(0,0).$\n\nFor the function $f$ to be continuous at $(0,0),$ the limit of the function must be equal to the function value, regardless of the path taken. So, we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{(x,y) \\to (0,0)}  f(x,y) = f(0,0) =0.\n$$\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. When we evaluate the limit along the path $y=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (0,0)\\,\\,y=x}f(x,y) & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{x\\cdot x}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function $f(x,y)$ is *not* continuous at the point $(0,0),$ even though both of its partial derivatives exist there." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124725 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
